{
  "analysis": "The review is a complaint about a specific bonus event ('the genie game') inside the casino slots app. The described mechanic is a wheel spin that stops on a large prize, after which the genie character forces the wheel onto another spin, and that follow-up spin pays far less than the prize that was shown. The reviewer frames this as recurring ('in multiple times'), not as a one-off glitch, and asks for a keep-or-spin choice, so the behavior is being described as a deliberate game rule rather than a bug. The central claim is that the game alters the result of a chance event after it has landed, taking away a displayed win and substituting a worse one. No real-money wager is described, so Gambling does not apply; the chance element itself is a wheel/spin reward delivery, which is a Reward Mania-style mechanic that a rigging claim does not nullify.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "in multiple times whe the wheel spin after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price, from 100 million with 5x multiplier to a 48 million",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The span asserts the game manipulates the determination of the wheel outcome: a prize already landed is taken away by forcing another spin that pays less, which is concealed manipulation of how results are decided."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "whe the wheel spin after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging / spin wheel delivery",
      "rationale": "A wheel spin is a chance-based delivery of prizes, and per the alleged-rigging boundary the reviewer's claim that the outcome is manipulated does not nullify Reward Mania, so both apply under R9."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is mostly a general complaint about time being wasted. The 30-second matchmaking wait is a service/queue time, which the codebook explicitly excludes from Playing by Appointment. The claim that rewards are 'all just for show' is a complaint about reward value, not about the delivery mechanism (no unpredictability, complexity, or collection pressure is described), so Reward Mania does not apply. The only concrete design behaviour described is that weapon upgrades and perks are difficult to obtain and the player must keep battling to win them, which is progression tied to sustained, repeated play. No payment, social, or collection mechanics are mentioned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Its difficult to get weapon upgrades or any percs others you stick battling to win",
      "rule_applied": "definition / vs Slow Progress boundary",
      "rationale": "The span names continued battling ('stick battling to win') as the required route to weapon upgrades and perks, so progress is gated behind sustained repeated play rather than merely described as slow."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing Clash Royale's late-ladder experience after 8,000 trophies. The concrete mechanics named are player-vs-player ladder matches (opponents running meta cards like Mega Knight, Pekka, and Golem that are hard to defend) and an explicitly asserted pay-to-win progression structure: the reviewer states that rising higher is not achievable with skill alone, implying money is the alternative route to rank up, and that this gets worse with updates. The balance complaints about card buffs/nerfs are quality complaints about the meta, not a described dark-pattern mechanic: nothing ties the card balance to devaluation of purchases or to manufactured scarcity. No temporal, psychological, or technical pattern is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win / Pay Wall (vs Free alternative)",
      "rationale": "The span asserts that reaching higher ranks is not achievable through skill alone, establishing that money is the path to progress, which is the paid-advantage/pay-wall arm of the pattern."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes the trophy-ladder mode where the player is matched against other players' decks, which is the game setting players in competition against each other."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review spans several distinct topics: a customer-service grievance about a 9-diamond purchase not producing a game element during play, an overall verdict that paying is unnecessary to win, a recommendation not to buy anything beyond 'the pass', and advice to recruit friends/a team to exchange lives. 'The pass' is the game's season pass, a recurring paid entitlement, named as the one thing worth buying and as the limit of future spending. The paid pass itself is the mechanic described. The advice to get friends and a team to trade lives describes a resource-exchange mechanic between people already playing, which is Reciprocity. The diamond purchase grievance names an in-game purchase but does not describe real money entering the currency, so it cannot tag Intermediate Currency. The statement that one need not pay to win describes the absence of a pattern and does not trigger Pay to Progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "you don't buy from this game beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes, MMO-subscriptions",
      "rationale": "The season pass is named as a paid entitlement the player can buy, and the reviewer advises limiting future purchases to it, so the recurring paid pass is present as the mechanic."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Get friends and a team and trade lives.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Players are told to swap lives with friends and teammates already in the game, which is the resource donation/exchange mechanic Reciprocity describes."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Fishdom game pass season pass what does it include",
  "search_result": "The Fishdom season pass is an optional paid entitlement granting boosters, coins, lives, and event-specific rewards, i.e. the recurring battle-pass structure."
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing the everyday loop of a match-3 game with limited lives and a daily log-in prize streak. Two existing mechanics surface through their suggestions. First, lives are refilled over a timer: the reviewer asks that a new life cost only 5-10 minutes, implying the current regen wait is longer and gates how much they can play. Second, daily prizes restart if a day is missed, which is a daily-login incentive with an attached penalty for absence. The complaint about 'small and unnecessary tasks like celebrating' is a vague quality gripe with no named progress-linked repeated activity, so it does not satisfy Grinding.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "The span names the life-refill timer as an existing gate and asks to shorten it, so play is paced by the game's regeneration clock rather than the player's choice."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in reward calendars",
      "rationale": "The review describes a daily prize calendar whose progress resets on a missed day, an incentive to log in daily with a penalty for failing to do so."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing in-game interstitial/video ads that they cannot dismiss. The ad screen has no close control, any tap anywhere navigates to a store page, returning to the game still leaves the ad's final frame stuck on screen, and the only escape is force-killing the app. This describes a design in which the player is compelled to sit through an ad (T_Advertisement) and, separately, an interface that obstructs the opt-out from that ad — no close control and a mis-tap surface that routes to a store (M_UIMisdirection). The ads are encountered inside the installed game app, so they are within scope under R13.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "there is no way to close it. No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store. If you go back to game it is still stuck in the last sceeen of the ad. You have to kill the game to come out of the ad screen",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The ad is shown inside the game and the player is compelled to view it because no dismissal exists and the only way out is killing the app, which is forced ad viewing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store",
      "rule_applied": "vs Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The close control is absent and any tap routes to a store, so the interface obstructs the opt-out from the ad and pushes interaction toward a purchase surface."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is a wholly positive, promotional-style account of the game. It praises graphics, storyline, and depth of strategy, and it names two concrete mechanics: building and expanding an empire, and real-time combat against other players. Building/expanding is described only at a high level with no timer, grind, or payment content, so no Temporal or Monetary label is supported. 'Kept me hooked' and 'captivating' are evaluations of engagement, not descriptions of an investment mechanism that raises the cost of leaving. The only concrete design behaviour named is player-versus-player combat, which the codebook's Competition label treats as the game pitting players against each other, irrespective of the reviewer's approval.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "definition / indicator: 'The game makes you compete against other players'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer names the game's player-versus-player combat as a present feature, so competition between players is implied by the mechanic being named, regardless of the positive evaluation."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
