  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that further progress is impossible without spending money, which is the Pay Wall instance of the pattern."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing the game's core loop. The energy system is the mechanic being discussed in detail: it is resource-gated, running out of energy stops further progress, and the recovery timer is long. That is a designed wait imposed by the game rather than the player's choice. The opening and closing remarks ('not that much to do', console wish, customization wish) are evaluations and requests for future features, not descriptions of active mechanics. No payment, social, or psychological mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "It's an Energy based game so once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully.",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "The span describes an energy gauge that refills on a long in-game timer and states that running out halts all progress, so play is forced onto the game's recovery schedule rather than the player's."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer alleges a causal manipulation: once they started buying Poké Balls with (presumably) real money, Pokémon began breaking out of captures more often, which consumes balls faster and forces further purchases. This is not a description of paywalls, timers, ads, currencies, or social mechanics; it is an assertion that the game secretly alters outcome determination (capture success) in response to purchases in order to drive spending. The appended edit disavowing authorship does not describe any mechanic and does not change what the main body claims about the game's design. The claim matches the alleged-rigging arm of Illusion of Control, which covers spans asserting concealed manipulation of outcomes rather than just an unlucky run.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The player asserts that capture outcomes were manipulated once they began paying, increasing ball consumption to force more purchases — a claim of concealed manipulation of how results are determined, not merely a bad run."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is about difficulty gating in Candy Crush. The first clause says hard levels can take a week to pass, but it names no repetitive activity the player must run, only that levels are hard and slow, so it is not Grinding. The second clause states that there are tasks that are impossible unless the player buys boosters, meaning the game reaches a point where progress is contingent on payment. The phrase 'unless you buy boosters' also sets the free route (impossible tasks) against a paid alternative, which is the Pay-to-Progress contrast.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / vs Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The span says tasks cannot be completed on the free path and that buying boosters is the way through, so progress is contingent on payment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes losing out on an almost-completed event reward because the game's event clock expired while they slept. They were close to earning 525 dice (three steps left), ran out of dice, the event still showed two days left, and after logging back in the event had been replaced. The core mechanic described is a time-limited event whose reward is forfeited when the player cannot keep to the game's schedule. There is no payment involved (they say they never purchased), no daily-login calendar, no repetitive task description, and no anticipatory pressure to keep playing—the player already quit and is describing the loss after the fact.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "The latest event I was about to get 525 dice as reward, I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "definition and FOMO boundary note",
      "rationale": "The event reward expired on the game's own clock while the player slept, and the span describes the schedule and the resulting loss, so play had to fit the game-defined window to keep the reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a session of MONOPOLY GO! after a season change and venting frustration across several mechanics. The friend-invite sentence names a recruitment design: the game ties rewards to the player inviting friends and getting them to join via Facebook; the complaint is that the reward has stopped since the new season, but the mechanic being described is the friend-invitation incentive itself. The dice sentence reports a bad run of chance events: playing at max bet and getting no shutdowns or bank heists. That is a losing run with no claim that outcomes are rigged, so it does not carry Illusion of Control, and no money is mentioned so it is not Gambling. The sticker sentence describes the randomized sticker-pack delivery: the reviewer keeps receiving duplicates instead of new cards, which is the duplicate-drop delivery experience of a randomised reward pool. The final sentences are a connection/login failure attributed to a technical fault, which R11 treats as a malfunction rather than a deliberate design pattern.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "Suddenly I get no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook.",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme",
      "rationale": "The game is described as rewarding the player for bringing friends into the game through Facebook, which is the friend-invitation incentive regardless of the reviewer complaining that the reward stopped this season."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way...",
      "rule_applied": "duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomised pool",
      "rationale": "The span describes how sticker rewards are delivered - repeated duplicates from the randomized sticker packs - rather than their value, which is the Variable Rewards arm of the definition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a design behaviour in the 1v1 matchmaking screen: every time the screen opens, the interface itself moves the cursor/selection onto the highest-stakes table the player can afford, instead of leaving the choice neutral or on a low-stakes table. The reviewer names this as a deliberate 'Dark UX' trick whose purpose is to make players accidentally tap and stake coins they did not intend to, so that after losing them they are pushed toward buying coins with real money. The described mechanism is entirely an interface manipulation: auto-scrolling, pre-selection, and a costly default the player must actively undo. The real-money outcome ('forced to buy real ones') is stated as the intended purpose of the interface behaviour, not as a separately described purchase flow, so the monetary lift is carried by the same UI mechanism.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Every time you open the game or go to the 1v1 section, it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins. This is a cheap \"Dark UX\" trick to force accidental mis-clicks so players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The interface is purposefully designed to steer the player's action onto a costly selected option against their intent, requiring an active opt-out, and the review explicitly states the purpose is to deplete coins and drive real-money top-ups."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing how the game's economy and randomness behave at higher levels. Three connected things are being described. First, the game's randomness is accused of being biased against the player ('the rng is so obviously against you'), which is an assertion that outcomes are manipulated rather than a report of an unlucky run. Second, playing without paying is described as unrewarding, establishing money as the alternative to a free path that falls short. Third, the specific economy fact is given: at around level 100 a level costs 2200 coins while winning barely returns that much, so the free route bleeds the player's funds faster than play can replenish them and spending becomes the only rewarding route. The reward complaint itself ('rewards need a big revamp') describes reward value, not a delivery schedule, so it does not trigger Reward Mania.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you it's crazy",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The span asserts that the game's randomness is tilted against the player, which is a claim of concealed, non-random outcome determination rather than a simple bad run."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's not rewarding playing the game without spending actual money.. at around level 100 it costs 2200 but if you win the level you'll barely make that back",
      "rule_applied": "vs Free alternative",
      "rationale": "Winning a level barely covers its entry cost, so the free route cannot sustain play and payment becomes the alternative needed to keep progressing rewardingly."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a base-builder whose events can only be won through very large spending. Their account of their own play implies the event structure is competitive: winning an event requires out-spending rivals, and skill is irrelevant for a 'general player.' 'Same old same old grinder' is a generic genre characterisation of the game rather than a description of a specific repetitive task the player must run, so it does not carry Grinding. The rest of the review is a pay-to-win complaint: general players have no chance in events, success is purchased, and the reviewer computes a 30k real-money outlay to win the last event. The closing wish is directed at other players' spending habits, not at a game mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to play. Zero skill pay to win. Calculated total spent on last event was 30k to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win",
      "rationale": "The span states directly that winning is purchased rather than earned by skill and quantifies the outlay, which is the Pay to Win instance of Pay to Progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "After a few weeks, it became abundantly clear that there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Winning an event in this context means beating other players in a competitive event, and the reviewer frames the event as a contest a non-spending player cannot win."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes four separate design behaviours after ten days of play. First, they name the monetisation model directly as pay-to-win. Second, base upgrade times growing to 'ridiculous' lengths is a build-timer gate: the player must wait on the game's clock to advance. Third, endless scaling of upgrade requirements with nothing to look forward to beyond more of the same work describes both an unattainable end state (treadmill) and the repetitive grind the path consists of. Fourth, they attribute the confusing, overwhelming interface to a deliberate purpose of pushing purchases, which is the interface working against the player's intent rather than a mere quality complaint. Finally, the alliance system is described as leaving players who do not join exposed to being attacked and bullied by other players, which pits players against each other in PvP; no reward or advantage flowing to the aggressor is described, so the stronger Encourages Anti-Social Behavior reading is not supported. No term in the review needs an external lookup.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win (low-level indicator of Pay to Progress)",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly names the model as pay-to-win, meaning paying grants an advantage over non-payers."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "Base upgrade/build timers force the player to wait on in-game timers, so play happens on the game's schedule rather than the player's."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rule_applied": "definition (no attainable end state)",
      "rationale": "Endless scaling with nothing ahead to aim for means the game continually extends beyond any possible completion."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "just more grind",
      "rule_applied": "alt. def. 'compelled to complete monotonous tasks in order to make progress'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer names the repetitive task work that endless scaling requires as the only thing awaiting them, tying grind to progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "boundary rule vs UI complaint (design attributed to a purpose against the player's intent)",
      "rationale": "The interface is described as intentionally confusing and overwhelming in order to steer the player into buying, which is UI serving a manipulative purpose rather than a quality flaw."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The game sets players against each other: those outside an alliance are preyed on by other players in PvP, so competition is the mechanic being described."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a change to the game's daily reward system: previously obtainable daily rewards are now gated behind a 50,000 wager, the free 'timed rewards' only yield up to 40,000, and the resulting shortfall can only be bridged by paying, which the reviewer refuses to do and so is quitting. The named and central mechanic is a daily task/reward system that the player must complete to obtain the day's reward, and failing or being unable to complete it forfeits the reward opportunity. The reviewer also draws the free-vs-paid contrast explicitly, implying the design routes completion of the daily rewards through payment because the free path cannot reach the threshold.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Daily task lists with resetting rewards",
      "rationale": "The daily reward is framed as a task (betting 50,000) that must be completed each cycle to obtain the reward, and being unable to complete it forfeits that day's reward opportunity, which is the daily-incentive-with-consequence structure."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "The most I can seem to get on the timed rewards is 40,000. I'd not pay just to get my daily rewards.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The reviewer contrasts the free route (timed rewards capping at 40,000) with paying to get the daily rewards, showing the free path falls short of the 50,000 threshold so completion is only smooth when payment is involved."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is about sticker-album collection and event progression in Dice Dreams. Three connected mechanics emerge. First, winning or completing anything meaningful is presented as contingent on large payments: winning 'anything' costs hundreds of dollars, and the last one or two stickers only arrive if you pay. That is progress gated behind money. Second, the sticker album is a completion set whose final pieces are withheld, so the player is held in the collection loop by the missing items. Third, the last stickers are described as never obtainable through normal play and only through payment, which describes an intentionally scarce set with a paid remedy sold to get around the scarcity. The statement about giving five stars only to be allowed to write the review concerns the app-store rating surface, which is outside the game application and not coded. The addiction warning is an evaluation rather than a mechanic description. No web search is needed because 'stickers' and 'goals' in this board-game context are self-explanatory collectible and event terms.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "if you want to win anything on this game it will cost hundreds of dollars. on the stickers you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "Winning and completing the sticker set are stated to be unavailable without large payments, so further progress is contingent on spending money."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The sticker album is a completion set and the withheld final pieces are what keep the player engaged and paying, which is the collection-compulsion pattern."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_DeceptiveLuxury",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "Remedy Consumption",
      "rationale": "The final stickers are said never to drop naturally, so their scarcity is built into the design and a paid route is offered to obtain them anyway, compelling an extra outlay for scarce items."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is a long-term player at level 8416 describing why they are quitting. Three design behaviours are present. First, the lives system: they run out of lives several times a day while trying to beat levels, which means sessions are cut short and play must resume only after lives regenerate on the game's clock. Second, progress difficulty: levels take days to beat and the reviewer states outright that buying add-ons is required to get through 'with a sane mind', and that the free path is a 'pure time waste' — progress is gated behind payment or excessive time. Third, they sum up time and money already spent as 'wasted' while uninstalling, which describes sunken cost but does not show that investment operating to keep them playing — they are leaving. The review names no currency conversion, no chance mechanics, no ads inside play, and no social features.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
